  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1113 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nShowers continue to trek across the mountains and northern tier of \nour forecast area, associated with the approaching upper-level \ntrough. As its axis shifts east, we may see a few embedded \nthunderstorms in the north/northeast plains after sunrise. Notably \ncooler temperatures can be expected across the lower elevations, \nwhere highs may struggle to break the 70 degree mark in the coolest \nareas (mainly the Palmer Divide and far NE plains). Most areas will \nsee reduced CAPE relative to previous days, limiting afternoon \nshower/t-storm coverage considerably. Have further reduced PoPs \nacross our mountains and urban corridor for this period, maintaining \nonly a very slight chance (15-20%) of precipitation. The axis of \nbetter instability (and shear) will be confined to our eastern \nplains, where a few stronger to marginally severe storms may develop.\n\nTransient ridging pushes in for Monday into Tuesday leading to a \ngradual rebound in temperatures and comparatively drier conditions. \nAn isolated shower or storm could make an appearance mainly south of \nI-70 Monday, and increasing southwest flow ahead of a developing \nclosed low over Nevada on Tuesday should promote a few isolated \nafternoon showers in the high country. That low is then slated to \nlift NE, merging with the broader flow pattern over the Northern \nRockies Wednesday, promoting a slight increase in showers and \nthunderstorms predominantly to our mountains, with more isolated \ncoverage elsewhere.\n\nBehind this, ensemble means favor steadily building high pressure \nover Colorado, supporting additional modest warming and potential \nfor temperatures to return to the 80's for some of the lower \nelevations for the second half of the week. Precipitation chances \nwill be fairly slim, but enough moisture could remain trapped \nbeneath the ridge to allow for isolated afternoon showers most\ndays.",
